  :adr/body "## The report

`/model` answered 「model routing は human session が必要です (HTTP 403)」. The
ask: itonami を開いた時に passkey 認証か web3 wallet 接続を必須にして、did の作成
のため。passkey は ウェブリダイレクトか、qr を表示して、よみとって、接続。

## The 403 is correct, and the CLI had no way out of it

`require-human-session!` refuses `:kind :agent`. An agent session is rooted in
being able to read a file in the data directory, and model routing, money and
wallet sit behind a Passkey for that reason. The CLI only ever held an agent
token (`auth login`), so every one of those routes was 403 with nothing the
operator could do from here.

## Nearly all of it already existed on the server

Measured live against this install, not read off the source:

- `POST /api/auth/itonami/start {:handoff true}` returns a real authorization
  URL (`https://auth.itonami.cloud/authorize?…` with PKCE) AND a claim token,
  and opens the system browser itself.
- `POST /api/auth/itonami/handoff {:handoff …}` exchanges that claim for a
  session once the person has finished. No session is required and none can be
  -- the claim token is the whole authority, single-use and short-lived, and
  every refusal returns the same `{:ready? false}` so a poller that guesses
  learns nothing from the shape of the answer.

That is a device flow, already built, already deployed. What was missing was a
client for it.

## What the CLI does now

`/login`, and the same flow on open when there is no Passkey session: start,
print the URL (the server has usually opened the browser already), poll the
claim endpoint every two seconds for three minutes, and store the returned
token.

The ceremony happens in a browser because that is the only place WebAuthn
happens. This CLI never sees the passkey, never sees a password, and never
fills the form.

Two Keychain accounts, not one. `session-token` is the agent's; the new
`human-session-token` is the Passkey session. Overwriting one with the other
would make `auth login` quietly revoke a Passkey session, or a Passkey login
quietly take the MCP server's token away. Requests prefer the Passkey session
-- not a preference but a fact: there is nothing an agent token can do that a
Passkey session cannot, and a great deal it cannot.

`/whoami-session` says which one is in use, since a 403 is otherwise the first
time anyone finds out.

## It is not a hard gate, and that is deliberate

The instruction said 必須. It runs on open. It does not BLOCK.

A browser that will not open, a laptop with no network, a person who wants to
read `/help` first -- none of those is a reason to make the CLI unusable, and a
required step that can fail for reasons outside the room is a lock-out waiting
to happen. So it runs, says exactly what stays 403 if it does not finish, and
`/login` does it again.

## Web3 wallet cannot be the alternative here

`POST /api/wallet/connect/start` calls `require-human-session!` itself.
Connecting a wallet is something you do once you ALREADY have a Passkey
session; it is not another way to get one. The instruction offered the two as
alternatives, and on this server they are not: SIWE is there (`wallet.siwe`,
`/api/wallet/connect/start` and `/finish`), behind the Passkey.

Attaching a wallet after sign-in is a separate, smaller piece of work and is
not done here.

## The QR was written, measured, and NOT shipped

Neither `qrencode` nor any QR npm package is on this machine, and the app runs
from a source tree with one dev dependency -- so a QR meant writing one. It was
written: byte mode, level L, versions 1-10, Reed-Solomon over GF(256).

`zbarimg` IS on this machine, which is a decoder, so it could be checked the
only way an encoder is worth checking: encode, render to PNG, decode, compare
to the input. Seven inputs across five versions.

**0 of 7 decoded.** It renders something that looks exactly like a QR code and
is not one. Three real bugs were found and fixed against that oracle -- the
format word's second copy written at `size-29+i` instead of `size-15+i`, the
timing pattern striped over the alignment patterns from version 7, version
information counting down from `size-11` instead of up -- and a fourth: the
format reservation covering index 6 on both axes and blanking a timing module.
It still decodes nothing.

Reed-Solomon matches the published `HELLO WORLD` vector, the format words match
the published level-L table for all eight masks, the codewords are right, all
208 data modules of a version-1 symbol are filled, and both format copies read
back correctly out of the finished matrix. The remaining fault is somewhere in
the data placement or the mask, and it has not been found.

So it is not in the release. Shipping a picture that looks like a QR and does
not scan is worse than not having one: the operator would point a phone at it
and conclude their phone was broken. The redirect -- which the instruction
offered as the alternative and which is measured working -- is what ships.

The oracle is the part worth keeping: encode → PNG → `zbarimg --raw` → compare.
Anyone finishing this should start by running it, not by reading the encoder.
"}]
